  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·09d</p>
    <h1>Little Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Little Raven (<em>Corvus mellori</em>) is an omnivore with a diet dominated by invertebrates — particularly insects — supplemented by seeds, grain, carrion, small vertebrates, and human food waste. It is a nomadic, flocking species that exploits seasonal insect abundance across south-eastern Australia's grasslands, farmland, and alpine zones.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on HANZAB species account:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, earthworms, moths, termites — ground-foraging for invertebrates is the primary foraging mode. The Little Raven exploits grasshopper and locust eruptions, moving nomadically to areas of insect abundance.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> wheat, barley, oats, and native grass seeds — particularly in agricultural areas. The species follows ploughing machines to take exposed grain and invertebrates.</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> roadkill (rabbits, kangaroos, lizards) and dead livestock. Less dominant in the diet than in the larger Australian Raven, but still an important supplement.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> skinks, mice, small birds and nestlings, frogs — taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> eggs of ground-nesting birds.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit:</strong> various berries and fruits taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> campsites, rubbish, urban scraps — exploited opportunistically.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nomadic Foraging and Insect Eruptions</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Little Raven is the most nomadic of the Australian corvids. Outside the breeding season it forms large flocks (sometimes hundreds of birds) that move across the landscape tracking food resources — particularly insect eruptions. During years of high grasshopper abundance in south-eastern Australia, Little Raven flocks can number in the thousands, exploiting the invertebrate resource across a wide area. This nomadic foraging strategy contrasts with the territorial year-round foraging of breeding Australian Ravens.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Alpine and High-Country Foraging</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Little Raven breeds in montane and alpine habitats of south-eastern Australia and the Australian Alps, where the summer insect season is short but productive. Alpine insects — particularly beetles and moths — are important dietary items during the breeding season in these high-altitude habitats. Post-breeding, flocks descend to lower-altitude farmland and open country for the cooler months.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
